  "narrative": {
    "intro": "California saw $42.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Federal buying in California included 188,421 awards with an average award size of $227K. Obligations fell 31.1% year over year. The Department of Defense was the largest buyer, obligating $22.6B, followed by the Department of Energy with $6.3B and the Department of Homeland Security with $4.8B. Research and development dominated industry spending, with Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) receiving $5.6B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences receiving $5.3B. Major vendors included LAWRENCE LIVERMORE NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC at $3.4B and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO at $2.5B. Contracting activity also funded Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $3.9B and Ship Building and Repairing at $2.4B. These figures reflect fiscal year to date obligations and therefore are partial totals for FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What agencies are the biggest buyers in California?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $22.6B. The Department of Energy obligated $6.3B and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $4.8B. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $2.7B and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $1.8B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the most federal dollars?",
        "body": "Research and development led industry spending with $5.6B and $5.3B in the two top R&D categories. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction captured $3.9B and Ship Building and Repairing captured $2.4B. LAWRENCE LIVERMORE NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC received $3.4B and FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO received $2.5B; other large vendors include NATIONAL STEEL AND SHIPBUILDING COMPANY at $1.9B and SPACE EXPLORATION TECHNOLOGIES CORP. at $1.7B."
      }
    ]
  },
